What is Active Directory?

Definition: Active Directory (AD) is a centralized system for managing users, computers, and resources on a Windows network.

Core Function: When you join a Windows domain, your computer and user account are managed by a Domain Controller (DC), which runs Active Directory.

Key Benefit: Instead of managing each computer individually, AD lets one team manage hundreds of computers from one place.

Example: At a company with 500 employees, instead of IT walking to each desk to create user accounts, they create one account in AD, and that person can log into any company computer.

Core AD Concepts (What You Learned)

1. Domain Controller (DC)
The server running Active Directory
Stores all user accounts, passwords, and permissions
Manages login authentication
You need at least one DC for a domain to work
Example domain name: THM.local

2. Users
Individual accounts (people who log in)
Each user has credentials (username + password)
Users can be organized into groups
User properties: Full name, email, department, job title, phone

3. Groups
Containers holding multiple users
Used to assign permissions to many people at once
Example groups: "Sales", "IT", "Finance", "Domain Admins"
Why groups matter: Instead of assigning file access to 50 sales people individually, you assign it to the "Sales" group once. When new people join Sales, they automatically get access.

4. Organizational Units (OUs)
Hierarchical containers organizing users and computers
Allow different policies for different departments
Example structure:
company.local
├── Sales
│ ├── Users
│ └── Computers
├── IT
│ ├── Users
│ └── Computers
└── Finance
├── Users
└── Computers
OUs can be nested (OUs within OUs)
Policies applied to one OU don't affect others

5. Group Policy (GPO)
Rules that enforce settings across computers/users
Applied to OUs
Examples of what GPOs can do:
Force password complexity (12 characters, uppercase, numbers)
Lock account after 5 failed password attempts
Force screen lock after 15 minutes inactivity
Deploy software automatically
Map network drives
Key benefit: Instead of visiting 100 computers to change settings, you change one GPO and it applies to all

6. Active Directory Users & Computers (ADUC)
The main tool for managing AD
Where you create users, groups, OUs
Where you link Group Policies
GUI interface (easier than command line)

7. Delegation
Granting specific permissions to users over OUs or objects
Example: "John manages all Sales users but can't touch IT users"
Follows principle of least privilege (people only get what they need)

8. Kerberos Authentication (Windows default)
The protocol users authenticate with on Windows networks
More secure than basic username/password
Uses tickets instead of passwords over network
Prevents passwords from being transmitted over network

9. NTLM (Older Authentication)
Legacy authentication method (older than Kerberos)
Still used for backwards compatibility
Less secure than Kerberos
Modern Windows defaults to Kerberos

10. Trust Relationships
Allow users in one domain to access resources in another domain
Example: Parent company domain trusts subsidiary domain
Used in multi-domain environments

11. Domain Forest
A collection of domains that share the same schema
Domains are organized in a tree structure
All domains in a forest share a trust relationship
Example: company.local (parent) and sales.company.local (child)

12. Machine Accounts
Accounts for computers (not people)
Every computer joined to domain has a machine account
Ends with $ symbol (e.g., DESKTOP-ABC123$)
Used for computer-to-domain communication

Typical AD Workflow
User Onboarding
HR submits new hire info (name, department, start date)
IT creates user account in appropriate OU (Sales, IT, etc.)
IT adds user to department group (Sales group, IT group)
User logs in for first time
GPOs automatically apply to user based on their OU
User gains access to department file shares via group membership

User Offboarding
Employee leaves company
IT removes user from all groups
IT disables or deletes user account
User can no longer log in to any computer
Resources automatically revoked (no manual per-computer cleanup)

Security in Active Directory
Password Policies (Applied via GPO)
Minimum length (e.g., 12 characters)
Complexity requirements (uppercase, lowercase, numbers, special chars)
Password history (can't reuse last 5 passwords)
Password expiration (must change every 90 days)
Account lockout (lock after 5 failed attempts for 30 minutes)

Principle of Least Privilege
Users get ONLY the permissions they need
Sales people can access Sales folder but NOT IT folder
Managers can reset passwords for their team but NOT other teams
Reduces damage if account is compromised

Audit Logging
AD logs all logins, password changes, account creations
Security teams review logs for suspicious activity
Compliance requirement (SOC 2, ISO 27001)

Real-World AD Uses
Enterprise Windows Networks — Every company with 50+ employees uses AD
Government Agencies — Manage access to classified information
Universities — Students use AD accounts to access email, resources
Hospitals — Patient data access controlled through AD
Banks — Highly restricted access based on role

